I want to upvote this. The prose was fine, the dialogue wasn't distracting, the story was clean and escalated in a way that made sense, and the anomaly itself was easy to understand without being reductive.

My problem is with the dealing of Dr Schrag's AroAce-ness. While the statements made by the director are clearly meant to illustrate how bad he is, the erasure of her orientation is portrayed as a mere fact of the anomaly, and I agree with Montauk House, but I think you could put it in the initial RAISA outline and it'd serve its purpose as an in-universe warning without disrupting the narrative in any way.

Currently a novote; I'll upvote once you add a content warning to the beginning.







